📝 Main Topics
1. MDTC V2 Architecture, Repository Alignment, and Pipeline Validation
Background:
The team needed to convert the manually deployed MFA-based setup into a reusable MDTC V2 deployment while separating infrastructure code from business application code and avoiding dependencies between application versions.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The target architecture comprises two stacks. The first stack combines account bootstrap, environment setup, and CodePipeline; updating it triggers pipeline execution. The second stack is deployed by the pipeline and contains API gateways, Lambdas, and other application resources. Separate application instances and versions should use isolated S3 buckets, roles, and related resources.
The initial acceptance milestone is a Hello World deployment before the pipeline is connected to full business logic. The test must demonstrate that code can move from Bitbucket to S3, an artifact can be retrieved from Artifactory, and a CloudFormation template can be deployed.
The initial source will be the MDTC/IAC-configured repository containing the pipeline configuration, described as an MDTC V2 pipeline repository forked from CloudCom or CloudCon. The test may be placed directly in that repository without business code and later mapped to the business repository. The final repository governance model separates business-team source code from a reusable central pipeline repository, with a possible second job to copy content between repositories.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
Rob will make the MDTC V2 pipeline deploy Hello World.
The deployment team will produce the unified template and align it with MDTC’s manifest, configuration, and repository structure.
Deepak will verify the IAC_ repository and Jenkins source configuration.
The team will preserve compatibility with the upstream reference architecture and minimize local changes and merge conflicts.
The existing manually deployed MFA setup will be converted into an MDTC-executable deployment by Rob and the deployment team.
Unresolved Matters:
The final selection and naming of the central and business-code repositories, the direction of the repository-copy job, and the required CFT and environment.yaml changes were not finalized.
2. Automated Artifactory Access and NHID Credential Management
Background:
The current deployment requires credentials and configuration files for NPM, PyPy, Docker, and other Artifactory access, but manual credential handling and embedded transformation logic would increase maintenance and upstream merge risk.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The long-term design will maintain one NHID username, one password, and one secret in Secrets Manager, using the rotating credential for Artifactory access. Required files, including NPMRC and Docker configuration, will be generated from the secret as needed. The transformation may use the existing Lambda mechanism or an equivalent reusable Pythonic utility; the utility approach is intended to isolate configuration logic from CodeBuild and support fully automated deployment without manual steps.
The Secrets Manager implementation built by Devinder Kaur’s team is the preferred direction, while the separate solution described by the deployment team is expected to be deprecated. Pardeep owns the Lambda function associated with NHID rotation, and rotation evidence must remain with the responsible NHID or vendor operation.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
Rob will develop the pip-installable utility and provide scripted usage for generating NPM, PyPy, and Docker configuration.
The utility is expected to maintain 100% test/code coverage.
The deployment team will confirm the implementation pattern with Anil and coordinate secret population and access with Devinder Kaur’s team.
The pipeline will consume the NHID secret at the build layer for Artifactory access.
The team will coordinate the required rotation Lambda and retain audit evidence for production and non-production rotation.
Unresolved Matters:
The exact Secrets Manager access requirements, including account and application-tag details, and the final choice between the Lambda transformation and the Pythonic utility remained open.
