4. Security Validation, Integration Testing, and Production Risk
Background:
The CloudFormation templates and pipeline-deployed code require security validation, and the team must confirm that the deployed solution works without regression.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
CloudFormation security scanning is separate from the Fortify scan for Docker containers. Findings include over-permissive KMS boundaries and other over-permissive configurations; Topaz considered the identified issues fixable and not severe enough to stop development. Code copied from Bitbucket and deployed through the pipeline will also require scanning.
After deployment and remediation, the team must perform substantial integration, end-to-end, and regression testing. Existing integration tests may be reused if they can validate the fully deployed solution.
Production progression depends on executive risk acceptance and prevention of data leakage. The work may be treated as a production milestone while testing begins in development, provided the implementation conforms to applicable standards, rules, and governance requirements.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
Topaz will continue remediating CloudFormation scan findings and maintain transparency on validation status.
The team will connect the Single Family deployment, MDTC deployer, and MDTC pipeline to enable integrated validation.
The team will identify the available integration tests or define an alternative end-to-end test approach.
The relevant executives will receive the validation and risk information required for a production decision.
Unresolved Matters:
The available integration-test coverage, the final end-to-end test method, and executive acceptance of residual production risk remained unresolved.
